# ── NetGuard local.rules — lab-specific, author-authored detection ──────────
#
# This file is for signatures tailored to THIS lab (the monitored-app, its
# topology) that Emerging Threats Open does not cover — the "author-authored
# detection" layer of plan §4.1.
#
# It is intentionally EMPTY right now (M3, first pass): the goal of this pass is
# to measure what ET Open catches on its own, before any gap-filling rules are
# written. The four self-referential Sprint-1 rules were moved to demo.rules and
# are excluded from scoring (ADR-003). Author rules land here in a later pass,
# each justified by a scorecard gap it closes.
#
# Detection coverage comes from ET Open (suricata.rules) plus the two
# author-authored rules below, which close measured gaps ET Open missed on
# its own (A09 C2 beaconing, A10 exfil — see the scorecard).
#
# Discipline: these match GENERALIZABLE attack characteristics that real
# malware shares, NOT the simulator's specifics. They deliberately do not
# match the /beacon or /upload paths the attack-sim happens to use — matching
# the sim's own strings is the self-referential trap that put the Sprint-1
# rules in demo.rules (ADR-003). A rule is only credible if it would fire on
# traffic the sim never sent.
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# A09 — C2 beaconing (T1071). Heuristic: repeated HTTP requests from a single
# host advertising a decade-obsolete "MSIE 6.0" user agent (a classic malware
# C2 tell) at a beacon-like rate. detection_filter fires only after several
# requests in a short window, modelling periodic check-ins rather than a single
# hit. Not true periodicity analysis (that needs behavioural analytics beyond a
# signature) — a documented heuristic. Benign lab traffic uses modern or
# tool-specific user agents, so this stays clear of the negative controls.
alert http $EXTERNAL_NET any -> $HTTP_SERVERS any (msg:"NETGUARD LOCAL C2 beacon heuristic - repeated legacy MSIE6 user-agent"; flow:established,to_server; http.user_agent; content:"MSIE 6.0"; nocase; detection_filter:track by_src, count 5, seconds 30; classtype:trojan-activity; sid:1000010; rev:1; metadata:author netguard, attack_target Server, mitre_technique_id T1071;)

# A10 — Exfiltration over HTTP (T1048). Heuristic: an inbound POST whose
# Content-Length is >= 1,000,000 bytes (7+ digits) — a suspiciously large
# outbound upload to the monitored server. Generalises to any bulk HTTP POST
# exfil, independent of URI. The benign "large download" control (N02) is a GET
# with a large *response*, not a large request body, so it does not match.
alert http $EXTERNAL_NET any -> $HTTP_SERVERS any (msg:"NETGUARD LOCAL large outbound POST - possible exfiltration"; flow:established,to_server; http.method; content:"POST"; http.header; pcre:"/^Content-Length\x3a\s*\d{7,}/mi"; classtype:policy-violation; sid:1000020; rev:1; metadata:author netguard, attack_target Server, mitre_technique_id T1048;)
